 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
 
-A request to process a list of strings can involve several operations: remove invalid entries, retain palindromes, and count the remaining items. Before writing the code for one of these operations, does a language model already represent which operation it is about to implement? We study this question at the level of a \emph{subgoal}: a meaningful intermediate operation that can admit multiple implementations. Our use of \emph{intent} refers to the identity of this upcoming operation, rather than to a subjective state or a complete plan for the program.
+\arnab{I am using terms like ``sub-goal'', ``intention'', ... interchangeably in the intro. We need to come to a consensus on what term to use.}
 
-Autoregressive prediction is compatible with representations of future computation. Prior work finds information about future tokens and constraints in model activations \citep{pal2023futurelens,lindsey2025biology}. Here we ask whether an upcoming subgoal is accessible as a semantic representation before its implementation appears, and whether transferring that representation can guide computation in a new context. Code provides a controlled setting in which the intended operation can be specified independently of its syntax and evaluated across programming languages.
+Consider a programmer asked to implement \emph{merge sort}, a task that decomposes into several sub-tasks: \emph{split the array}, \emph{sort the two halves}, and \emph{merge the sorted halves}. Before writing the loop that combines the two sorted arrays, the programmer already has a coherent goal: \textit{``merge the two halves.''} We ask whether large language models (LLMs) write code the same way: when an LLM emits a long span of tokens to satisfy a multi-step specification, does it hold an explicit representation of the \emph{next} instrumental sub-goal before it begins its implementation?
 
-Our main experiments use \texttt{Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct} on sequential predicate-implementation tasks. We identify attention heads whose output contributions transfer a predicate between contexts with different programming languages. We then evaluate these heads on multi-step prompts at positions around the beginning of the next implementation. The paper develops three findings.
+We study the \emph{instrumental} level of this hierarchy: the \emph{broader goal} (implement merge sort) decomposes into \emph{instrumental sub-goals} (merge the two sorted arrays), each of which admits many \emph{implementations} (such as using a \emph{for} loop or a \emph{while} loop). Our question concerns a representation of \emph{which sub-goal the model is about to pursue}, and whether that representation can be separated from how it is realized in code.
 
-\paragraph{Upcoming subgoals are readable and transferable.}
-\goalscope extracts selected-head contributions at a source position and injects their aggregate into a separate model run with a fixed verbalization prompt (\Cref{sec:what}). Its continuation describes the upcoming operation. Injecting the signal into a code stub instead tests whether it can induce implementation in another programming language. These complementary readouts test semantic accessibility and behavioral transfer; neither requires the source code to be supplied as text to the probe.
+Although LLMs are trained to predict one token at a time, a growing body of work shows that they \emph{look ahead}: they encode information about tokens they have not yet emitted \citep{pal2023futurelens} and plan toward constraints several tokens in the future \citep{lindsey2025biology}. We ask what the plan for the \emph{next step} looks like as a concrete internal object, and whether that object is one we can read and move between contexts.
 
-\paragraph{Sparse readout and native causal involvement differ.}
-A small head set recovers much of the readout performance of the full selection, but a broader intervention is needed to strongly disrupt native implementation (\Cref{sec:heads}). We distinguish these findings with controlled span-restriction and sink-attention experiments. The results identify a causally important route for task information without claiming that the selected heads alone constitute the entire subgoal computation.
+We investigate this question through activation patching on code tasks with multiple sub-goals. Our main experiments use \texttt{Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct}, with replications across model families and sizes. Our analysis yields three key findings.
 
-\paragraph{Subgoal information is available in both attention and the residual stream.}
-The heads attend to relevant task descriptions, and changing their task-span keys and values can redirect implementation. Query transfer can also favor a particular source subgoal (\Cref{sec:mechanism}). An OV-based transformation makes subgoal information readable from intermediate residual states. Together, these results constrain how the representation interacts with context while leaving its precise computation open.
+\textbf{The next sub-goal is encoded before implementation, and is verbalizable (\Cref{sec:what}).} We localize the encoding of the LM's next sub-goal to a compact set of attention heads, which we call the \goalheads. We introduce \goalscope, a lightweight probe built on these heads that verbalizes the upcoming sub-goal directly from the LM's internal activations. Before the model writes the implementation, we can extract what the heads write to the residual stream and use it to generate a faithful description of the operation it is about to perform.
 
-We also examine few-shot relation tasks and free-form code to explore the reach of the code-localized readout (\Cref{sec:generalization}). Replications within other model families and training regimes are reported in \Cref{app:model_comparison}. These are replications of the phenomenon within each model, rather than transfers of activations between models. Our central claim is that an upcoming subgoal has a readable and manipulable representation before its implementation, not that the model forms its entire plan in advance or represents that plan in natural language.
+\textbf{The encoding is abstract and portable (\Cref{sec:what:readout}).} Transplanting the encoding steers a fresh code context to pursue the source sub-goal. A goal extracted from Python can trigger the same operation in C++, and vice versa. This portability provides evidence that the heads carry a representation of the operation that generalizes across its surface realizations. We also explore whether the same heads can verbalize sub-goals in code whose component steps are not explicitly listed, and inferred relations in non-code few-shot tasks (\Cref{sec:generalization}).
+
+\textbf{Goal heads mediate the model's implementation of the sub-goal (\Cref{sec:heads,sec:mechanism}).} A small set of heads suffices for effective readout, and a broader set is causally important for carrying out the operation. When we restrict other upper-layer heads' access to the upcoming task and code span, preserving goal-head access largely preserves implementation performance. Conversely, restricting the broader goal-head set to the attention sink sharply reduces performance, while restricting random heads has little effect. The heads attend to relevant task descriptions, and changing their query or key/value states can redirect the sub-goal pursued. Using their value and output projections, we also introduce \textbf{OVReader} to trace where the upcoming sub-goal becomes readable in intermediate residual states.
+
+Our results provide concrete, testable evidence that code LLMs represent and pursue intermediate goals: a compact, portable, causally manipulable encoding of \emph{intent} that precedes action.
